programs.haiku:
"""Word-class guess for a weak, diffuse component.

Its activation is mostly small noise (std about 0.4) with no simple token rule behind
it. The clearest word-level tendencies, which this program writes down:
- Japanese/Korean object and place case particles (を 을 를, に へ で 에) push it negative,
  most strongly for the object markers.
- Appearance and feel words (look, looks, smooth, texture, flavor, feel, want) push it negative.
- Adverse-outcome words (deaths, mortality, survival, killed, murder, enemy) push it positive.
- Modal and focus words (can, may, will, also, only, since) lean slightly positive.
Everything else sits at the component's mean, 0.03.
"""

READS = ()

OBJECT_PARTICLES = {"を", "을", "를"}
PLACE_PARTICLES = {"に", "へ", "で", "에"}
APPEARANCE = {" look", " looks", " looked", " smooth", " texture", " flavor", " feel",
              " want", " wants", " taste", " appearance"}
OUTCOME = {" deaths", " death", " mortality", " survival", " enemy", " attacks", " killed",
           " murder", " losses", " outcomes", " infection", " fatal"}
MODAL_FOCUS = {" can", " may", " will", " also", " only", " since", " either"}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    token = prefix.token_strings[-1]
    if token in OBJECT_PARTICLES:
        value = -1.0
    elif token in PLACE_PARTICLES:
        value = -0.6
    elif token in APPEARANCE:
        value = -0.5
    elif token in OUTCOME:
        value = 0.6
    elif token in MODAL_FOCUS:
        value = 0.2
    else:
        value = 0.0
    return carry, 0.03 + value

"""Predicts the activation of MLP down_proj component 1039 in layer 15 of Qwen3-1.7B.

What the component is: one rank-one piece of layer 15's MLP output projection. Its input
direction reads mostly one MLP hidden unit (1039) and writes into the residual stream. Its
activation is small and diffuse (mean 0.02, standard deviation 0.27), and it is silent on the
first token of each document.

What this program does: it predicts a small value per token from what the token is, in plain
classes, and 0 at the first token of each document. Chat-template markers and math symbols
(√, ^, ')/') push it up; digits push it up a little; the words 'and', 'as', 'to' push it down;
other tokens sit at the average. These classes are a weak description: over 8 million training
tokens they explain only about 0.2% of the variance, since the activation depends on the
whole context and not on the token alone.
"""

READS = ()

MEAN = 0.02
CHAT_MARKERS = {"<|im_start|>", "<|im_end|>", "<think>", "</think>", "<|endoftext|>"}
MATH_SYMBOLS = {"√", "^", ")/", ")^"}
JOINERS = {"and", "as", "to"}


def value_of(token):
    word = token.strip()
    if word in CHAT_MARKERS:
        return 0.3
    if word in MATH_SYMBOLS:
        return 0.2
    if len(word) == 1 and word.isdigit():
        return 0.06
    if word in JOINERS:
        return -0.05
    return MEAN


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    ids = prefix.document_ids
    if len(ids) == 1 or ids[-1] != ids[-2]:
        return carry, 0.0  # first token of a document: the component is silent
    return carry, value_of(prefix.token_strings[-1])

"""Layer 15 MLP hidden unit 1049 (the component's input direction is about one-third this unit).

In plain terms: the unit dips negative when the text is set up for a bare verb that an
ability or opportunity word calls for: "opportunity to", "able to" (mean about -0.28 after
these words). "could", "can" and "allows" are negative only on average, and only slightly.
It leans mildly positive after "avoid", "make" and "because". Everywhere else it sits near its
average, a little above zero. The same words often flip sign in other contexts, and no word list
explains most of the variance, so this rule captures almost none of it. It describes the
strongest tails, not a model of the unit.
"""

ABILITY = {
    "able", "ability", "can", "could", "cannot", "opportunity", "chance", "enough",
    "allow", "allows", "allowing", "enable", "enables", "enabling", "help", "helps",
}
PURPOSE = {"because", "avoid", "make"}
MEAN = 0.0128  # the unit's average value, the default everywhere else
READS = ()  # reads no other component


def word(text):
    return text.strip().lower()


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    cur = word(prefix.token_strings[-1])
    same_doc = len(prefix.document_ids) > 1 and prefix.document_ids[-2] == prefix.document_ids[-1]
    prev = word(prefix.token_strings[-2]) if same_doc else ""
    if cur == "to" and prev in ABILITY:
        value = MEAN - 0.3  # "opportunity to", "able to": a bare verb is due next
    elif cur in PURPOSE:
        value = MEAN + 0.1
    else:
        value = MEAN
    return carry, value

"""Layer-15 MLP hidden unit 1059, read as a question-opening detector.

The component's activation is almost exactly this unit's value (it carries about 40% of the
component's variance). The unit goes negative on the words that open a question and build its
inverted word order, and mildly positive on the question mark that closes it:

  * a wh-word that starts a sentence ("What", "Why", "How", "Who"): negative;
  * a sentence-initial auxiliary ("Are", "Does", "Was", "Do", "Can"): negative;
  * the auxiliary right after a sentence-initial wh-word ("What [do] you", "Why [did]"):
    strongly negative;
  * the subject pronoun right after a sentence-initial auxiliary ("Are [you]", "Do [I]"):
    negative;
  * a "?" token: slightly positive;
  * 吗, the Chinese yes/no question particle: positive;
  * a Spanish opening "¿": negative;
  * chat and tool markup tags such as <|im_start|> or <tool_call>: slightly positive.

Everything else predicts 0. The rule is causal: it reads the sentence so far, resets at each
sentence end (. ! ? newline) and at each document boundary, and never looks ahead.

Scope: these rules explain only about 1-2% of the component's variance. Most of its variance is
a broad, diffuse body of small values across ordinary text that no simple word rule captures.
"""

import re

WH_WORDS = {"what", "why", "how", "who", "whom", "whose", "where", "when", "which"}
AUX_WORDS = {"is", "are", "was", "were", "do", "does", "did", "can", "could", "will",
             "would", "should", "has", "have", "had", "may", "might", "shall", "am"}
PRONOUNS = {"i", "you", "we", "he", "she", "it", "they"}
END_MARKS = (".", "!", "?", "\n")

READS = ()

WH_START = -2.0       # wh-word opening a sentence
AUX_START = -1.5      # auxiliary opening a sentence (yes/no question)
AUX_AFTER_WH = -3.3   # auxiliary right after a sentence-opening wh-word
PRON_AFTER_AUX = -1.5  # subject pronoun right after a sentence-opening auxiliary
QUESTION_MARK = 0.3   # "?" token
PARTICLE = 1.5        # 吗, the Chinese yes/no question particle
MARKUP = 0.3          # chat and tool markup tags such as <|im_start|>, <tool_call>


def _word(token):
    return re.sub(r"[^a-z]", "", token.lower())


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    token = prefix.token_strings[-1]
    word = _word(token)
    new_doc = len(prefix.token_ids) == 1 or prefix.document_ids[-1] != prefix.document_ids[-2]
    # state: tokens so far in this sentence, and the sentence's first word
    k, first = (0, "") if carry is None or new_doc else carry
    value = 0.0
    if k == 0 and word in WH_WORDS:
        value = WH_START
    elif k == 0 and word in AUX_WORDS:
        value = AUX_START
    elif k == 1 and first in WH_WORDS and word in AUX_WORDS:
        value = AUX_AFTER_WH
    elif k == 1 and first in AUX_WORDS and word in PRONOUNS:
        value = PRON_AFTER_AUX
    if token.strip() == "?":
        value = QUESTION_MARK
    elif token.strip() == "吗":
        value = PARTICLE
    elif token.strip() == "¿":
        value = WH_START
    elif re.fullmatch(r"<[^>]*>", token.strip()):
        value = MARKUP
    if k == 0:
        first = word
    if any(mark in token for mark in END_MARKS):
        carry = (0, "")
    else:
        carry = (k + 1, first)
    return carry, value

"""Layer 15 MLP down_proj component 1068: a readout of the token's type and its predecessor's.

This component is diffuse. Its value varies a lot from one position to the next, and most of
that variation depends on the wider context rather than on the token's type alone, so a
readable program captures only part of it. The program predicts the sum of two means:
  - OWN: how much a token of each type adds, given the token itself, and
  - BEFORE: how much it adds when the token just before it was of each type.
Chat and tool markup (<|im_start|>, user, <tool_call>, </think>, ...) is by far the strongest,
then dotted code calls (.put, .List) and colons. The first token of each document is always
0, where the component is off.
"""
import re

READS = ()

CHAT = {"<|im_start|>", "<|im_end|>", "user", "assistant", "<tool_call>", "</tool_call>",
        "<tool_response>", "</tool_response>", "<think>", "</think>", "<|endoftext|>"}

OWN = {"chat": 3.3, "dot": 1.9, "colon": 0.8, "code": 0.5, "punct": 0.2, "text": 0.1,
       "space": 0.1}
BEFORE = {"chat": 2.2, "space": 0.75, "colon": 0.8, "dot": 0.7, "digit": 0.5, "punct": 0.55,
          "code": 0.45, "newline": 0.35, "capword": 0.35, "text": 0.15}


def kind(s):
    """The type of one token's text."""
    if s.strip() in CHAT:
        return "chat"
    if s.strip() == ":":
        return "colon"
    if re.fullmatch(r"\.[A-Za-z_]\w*", s):
        return "dot"
    if re.fullmatch(r"\s?[a-z]+_[a-z_]+|\s?[A-Za-z]+[A-Z]\w*", s):
        return "code"
    if s.strip() == "":
        return "space"
    if "\n" in s:
        return "newline"
    if re.fullmatch(r"\s?\d+", s):
        return "digit"
    if re.fullmatch(r"\s*[^\w\s]+", s):
        return "punct"
    if re.fullmatch(r"\s?[A-Z][a-z]+", s):
        return "capword"
    return "text"


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    if len(prefix.document_ids) < 2 or prefix.document_ids[-1] != prefix.document_ids[-2]:
        return carry, 0.0
    own = OWN.get(kind(prefix.token_strings[-1]), 0.0)
    before = BEFORE.get(kind(prefix.token_strings[-2]), 0.0)
    return carry, own + before
